    "answer": {
      "short": "Yes. Both methods always produce primitive Pythagorean triples.",
      "worked": "Pythagoras’ method gives a second side and hypotenuse that are consecutive integers. Any common factor of all three sides would divide these consecutive integers, so it must be 1.\n\nFor Plato’s method, write the starting side as $4k$, where $k$ is a positive integer. The other sides are $4k^2-1$ and $4k^2+1$. They are odd and differ by 2. Any common factor of all three sides must divide 2, and it must also be odd. Hence it is 1.\n\nTherefore both methods produce primitive Pythagorean triples.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
